 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 639825,
   "is_answer_order_important": true,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "The human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) reproduces very quickly. A single virus can replicate itself a billion times in one 24-hour period. In a hypothetical treatment situation, a patient’s HIV population consists entirely of drug-resistant viruses after just a few weeks of treatment. How can this treatment result best be explained? How does this explanation illustrate that evolution is an ongoing process?",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1246816,
     "content_html": "The resistant viruses passed their genes to the non-resistant viruses so that 100% of the viruses became resistant. This illustrates evolution as an ongoing process because the genes of the population changed in real time."
    },
    {
     "id": 1246819,
     "content_html": "The non-resistant viruses died, and the resistant ones survived and rapidly reproduced. This illustrates evolution as an ongoing process because the change in the HIV population is the result of natural selection."
    },
    {
     "id": 1246817,
     "content_html": "The viruses developed resistance to the drug after repeated exposure to it. This illustrates evolution as an ongoing process because the viruses were able to adapt to changing conditions."
    },
    {
     "id": 1246818,
     "content_html": "The drug-resistant viruses were more fit than their non-resistant counterparts to begin with, and over time they dominated the population. This illustrates evolution as an ongoing process because natural selection favored one phenotype over another."
    }
   ],
